---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';

const facts = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Design and facilitation' },
  { label: 'Org', value: 'Discovery Education' },
  { label: 'Started', value: 'March 2025' },
  { label: 'Outcome', value: 'Adopted beyond design' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="A system that outlived its author · Jessica Loredo"
  description="A competency framework and self-assessment worksheet for designers that HR borrowed to build their own, now used in reviews, goal setting, and promotion discussions."
  eyebrow="Case study · Designer growth framework"
  headline="A system that outlived its author"
  lede="Designers had no way to see their own growth except through a manager's opinion of it. So I built the instrument, and then HR built theirs from mine."
  facts={facts}
>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the problem</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Growth measured by vibes</h2>
      <p>
        Before this existed, a designer at Discovery Education had no structured way to assess their
        own skill level. Career conversations ran on qualitative feedback and whatever their manager
        happened to notice. HR had goal-setting initiatives, but they were general enough that they
        couldn't measure design skill progression at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Managers had the mirror-image problem. Asked to guide someone's professional development
        beyond "ship the roadmap" or "be more senior," they had nothing concrete to point at. And
        because HR had no design-specific framework, advancement decisions rested on impressions,
        which is exactly the condition under which advancement stops being equitable.
      </p>
      <PullQuote>
        If nobody can describe what better looks like, then better is whatever the person in the
        room says it is.
      </PullQuote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the approach</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Starting from someone else's work</h2>
      <p>
        I didn't invent a competency model from nothing. There was a well-regarded public framework
        for design teams already, and starting there meant the arguments could be about what was
        missing rather than about whether a framework was needed.
      </p>
      <p>Two things were missing for how our team actually worked:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Influence.</strong> The soft skills and leadership behaviors that determine whether
          a designer's work lands on a cross-functional product team. This was inspired by an
          unfinished framework from a former manager, which deserves the credit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Analysis.</strong> Synthesizing research and information into a point of view,
          which is arguably the core designer skill and was nowhere in the original.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The original was also rigid, which was the third gap. A researcher and a visual systems
        specialist should not be measured on an identical column set, so the model needed to flex
        by specialization.
      </p>
      <p>
        I socialized it with managers and senior designers first, then introduced it in a Design Jam,
        then took it deeper in the K-12 team meeting. The questions that came back were mostly about
        seniority mapping and how it would reconcile with corporate HR systems, which told me
        something important: people were treating it as real before it was official.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the insight</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>A document nobody touches is a document nobody uses</h2>
      <p>
        Reading through it, I could see the gap. It was a reference table. Designers were already
        asking how to track themselves against it, and my read was that they'd want to interact with
        the framework rather than consult it. A static rubric gets looked at once.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I designed a worksheet set: self-assessment, structured space to cite real examples of
        your own work as evidence, and manager feedback in the same artifact. Then radial
        visualizations, so a designer could see the shape of their skills instead of reading a grid.
        The picture is what makes it land. You can see your own asymmetry immediately.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="The competency matrix, the radial skill visualizations, and the self-assessment worksheets."
        needs="Growth framework artifacts: the skills matrix, radial charts in light and dark, and worksheet examples."
        ratio="16 / 10"
      />
      <p>
        This is the same conviction that runs through everything else on this site. Engagement is
        not a nice-to-have on top of a system. It's the difference between a system that gets used
        and one that gets archived.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">iteration</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Three passes</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>First</strong>, the framework itself: refining the wording at every category and
        competency intersection to match how our design org actually talked, then piloting it with a
        small group of designers during quarterly reviews.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Second</strong>, usability. The radial charts and the worksheets came out of this
        round, along with a dark mode, plus layout and structure fixes that came directly from
        watching people try to fill them in.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Third</strong>, specialization, which is still in progress. The goal is swappable
        competencies so a research-focused designer can trade aesthetic criteria for deeper research
        ones without leaving the framework.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most useful feedback came from use, not from review. People agreed with the framework in
        the abstract and only discovered what was ambiguous when they had to score themselves
        against it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">outcome</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>It left the design team</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>HR borrowed elements of this framework to build their own.</strong> A tool made for
        one design org became the basis for how the company thought about growth more broadly. I had
        no authority over HR and no mandate to influence them. The framework did that on its own,
        because it was more concrete than what existed.
      </p>
      <p>It's in active use for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Performance reviews</li>
        <li>Goal setting and career development planning</li>
        <li>Promotion discussions</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The outcome I think about most, though, is a single case. A designer on our team
        participating in an on-the-job training program for professionals on a work visa needed
        formal documentation of her skills and development plan. There was no standardized way to
        produce that. The framework became it.
      </p>
      <PullQuote>
        A competency rubric is an abstraction until the day it's the only document that can show
        what someone is capable of.
      </PullQuote>
      <p>
        Designers also changed their plans because of it. One realized they wanted to grow in
        strategic thinking and set a goal against it. Others found their self-assessment didn't
        match the criteria they'd assumed they met, and adjusted toward things they'd been
        overlooking. That gap between self-perception and definition is the whole point.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">still open</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>What isn't finished</h2>
      <p>
        Formal validation with HR so it can carry weight in promotion decisions is still in
        progress. That's the difference between a tool people find useful and one that functions as
        objective evidence, which is what would make advancement genuinely more transparent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Specialization tracks aren't built. Wording still needs refinement, and the bi-annual team
        and culture survey now includes questions on career pathing so the framework keeps getting
        revised against what people actually report.
      </p>
      <p>
        The thing I'd tell anyone building one of these: we approached it like a design problem,
        prototype and test and refine, and that was right. But no amount of review surfaced what
        real usage surfaced in a week.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
